First Author: Rodriguez
Title: A Framework for Using Real-World Data and Health Outcomes Modeling to Evaluate Machine Learning–Based Risk Prediction Models
Source number: 42  ￼

Very short summary: This study proposed a real-world-data microsimulation framework to evaluate the clinical utility of a machine-learning risk model for lung transplant referral in adults with cystic fibrosis. Five key findings were: (1) the simulation cohort included 4,247 adults from the Cystic Fibrosis Foundation Patient Registry validation set; (2) the ML model predicted 2-year mortality and had better baseline discrimination than FEV1 alone, with AUROC 0.914 versus 0.876; (3) over 5 years, referral rates were 12% under usual care, 19% under FEV1-based referral, and 20% under ML-based referral; (4) among 309 patients who died before referral under usual care, 40% would have been referred under ML versus 31% under FEV1; and (5) despite earlier and more frequent referrals, fixed organ supply meant there were no significant differences in transplants or 5-year overall survival across policies. Figures 1–4 and Table 1 were directly used for this extraction.  ￼

