Section 6: Quality Assessment (Risk of Bias / PROBAST)
	•	Participants (Selection Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The study used a large national CFF registry linked to UNOS, covering a substantial proportion of the U.S. cystic fibrosis population, which reduces major center-level selection bias.  ￼
	•	Predictors (Input Variable Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Predictors were longitudinal real-world clinical variables collected at repeated encounters and used dynamically over time, which closely matches intended clinical use.  ￼
	•	Outcome (Definition Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Outcomes were clearly defined, including referral, transplant, pretransplant death, post-transplant death, and 5-year overall survival, with explicit state transitions in the microsimulation model.  ￼
	•	Analysis (Modeling Bias): Unclear | Quote/Reasoning: Strengths include linked real-world registries, dynamic decision modeling, patient-level microsimulation, and explicit accounting for organ supply and heterogenous treatment effects. Uncertainty remains because many downstream effects were simulated rather than directly observed, and results depend on structural assumptions such as organ allocation and post-transplant survival modeling.  ￼
	•	Applicability to Review Question: High Concern | Quote/Reasoning: Although the paper is highly relevant to lung transplantation and ML-based referral decisions, the population is adults with cystic fibrosis rather than a general thoracic transplant candidate population, and the article is mainly a methodological framework rather than a standard transplant prediction-model development paper.  ￼

